<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Tajikistan</h1><p class="st-meta">1991 CE – present · capital Dushanbe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The only Persian-speaking state of former Soviet Central Asia, which recovered from a devastating civil war under the long rule of Emomali Rahmon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tajik-ssr">Tajik SSR</a> declared independence on 9 September 1991. Tension between the old communist elite, largely from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulob">Kulob</a>, and an opposition of democrats, Islamists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamiris">Pamiris</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gharm">Gharm</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorno-badakhshan-autonomous-region">Gorno-Badakhshan</a> erupted into civil war in 1992.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The civil war of 1992 to 1997 killed tens of thousands and displaced hundreds of thousands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emomali-rahmon">Emomali Rahmon</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulob">Kulob</a>, became head of state in 1992 and president in 1994. A UN-brokered peace agreement in 1997 brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-tajik-opposition">United Tajik Opposition</a> into government, but Rahmon later sidelined it and banned the Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> Party in 2015. The economy depends on aluminium, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, hydropower such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogun-dam">Rogun dam</a>, and remittances from migrants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Tajikistan remains a presidential republic dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emomali-rahmon">Rahmon</a> and his family, with close security ties to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and growing Chinese investment, and periodic border clashes with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> over disputed enclaves.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tajikistan.jpg" alt="Tajikistan" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tajikistan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1991 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dushanbe</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rahmon Nabiyev, Emomali Rahmon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tajik</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Tajikistan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=tajikistan" aria-label="Tajikistan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6388 793 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tajikistan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6708 1049L6708 1050"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6708 1050h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6708 1049h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6708 1049h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dushanbe. <a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=tajikistan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
